TOPIC: Representation of DataIntroductionRepresentation of data involves organizing raw information into tables, charts, and graphs so that patterns, trends, and comparisons become easy to see and understand. In JAMB, the focus is not on drawing these representations but on reading, interpreting, and extracting correct information from them quickly and accurately.For a complete beginner:
Think of data representation as "telling a story with pictures or tables".  A table shows exact numbers  
A histogram/bar chart/pie chart shows the story visually
JAMB gives you the chart/table and asks questions like: "Which is the highest?", "How many in this range?", "What percentage?", etc.

This topic is extremely high-scoring in JAMB Mathematics – usually 4–8 questions every year.
Most questions are direct reading and simple calculations – candidates who practice careful reading score full marks here.World-standard learning objectives (aligned with Cambridge IGCSE, WAEC, NECO, IB MYP, and international standards):
Candidates should be able to:Read and interpret frequency distribution tables (grouped/ungrouped), identifying class intervals, frequencies, modal class, and ranges
Extract accurate information from histograms (continuous data), including frequencies, modal class, and comparisons
Interpret bar charts (discrete/categorical data) to compare categories, find totals, and calculate differences
Read pie charts to determine proportions, percentages, and actual values from angles or sectors
Solve real-life interpretation problems involving tables and charts (e.g., exam scores, survey results, population data)

Here are very clear visual aids to help you master data representation:This picture shows a complete frequency distribution table with class intervals, tally, and frequency columns.This histogram example shows touching bars for continuous data with frequency on y-axis.This bar chart displays separated bars for discrete categories with clear labels.This pie chart illustrates sectors with angles and percentages clearly marked.This diagram compares histogram (touching bars) vs bar chart (separated bars) – very important for JAMB.SUBTOPIC 1: Frequency DistributionObjective 1: Identify a Frequency Distribution TableExplanation:
A frequency distribution table organizes data to show how often each value or class of values occurs.  Ungrouped: lists individual values and their frequencies  
Grouped: uses class intervals (e.g., 20–29, 30–39) for large data sets

Key Points:Class interval → range of values (e.g., 40–49)  
Frequency → number of times values in that class occur  
Total frequency → total number of observations  
Modal class → class with highest frequency

Visual Type: table-structure
Visual Items:  Columns: Class interval | Tally | Frequency  
Highlighted modal class

Example Objective Question Patterns (JAMB style):Which column shows the number of students scoring between 50–59 marks?
(Answer: Frequency column for class 50–59)
In the grouped frequency table, what is the modal class?
(Answer: Class with highest frequency)
How many observations are there in total?
(Answer: Sum of all frequencies)

Common Mistakes:Confusing class interval with frequency  
Adding class limits instead of frequencies

Objective 2: Interpret Information from a Frequency TableExplanation:
Interpretation means reading the table correctly to answer specific questions without calculation errors.Key Points:Number in a range → add frequencies of relevant classes  
Modal class → highest frequency  
Cumulative frequency → running total (sometimes asked)

Visual Type: highlight-table
Visual Items:  Highlighted range (e.g., 50 and above)  
Modal class circled

Example Objective Question Patterns:How many candidates scored 60 marks and above?
(Answer: Sum frequencies of classes 60–69, 70–79, etc.)
What is the total number of students in the survey?
(Answer: Grand total of frequencies)
Which score range has the least number of students?
(Answer: Class with smallest frequency)

Common Mistakes:Forgetting inclusive class limits (e.g., 50–59 includes 50 and 59)  
Selecting only one class instead of the full range

SUBTOPIC 2: HistogramObjective 3: Interpret a Histogram CorrectlyExplanation:
A histogram displays continuous grouped data using bars that touch each other.  Height of bar = frequency  
Width of bar = class interval width  
Area of bar is proportional to frequency (important when class widths vary)

Key Points:Bars touch → no gaps (continuous data)  
Read frequency from y-axis  
Modal class → tallest bar

Visual Type: bar-diagram
Visual Items:  Touching bars with frequency labels  
Modal class highlighted

Example Objective Question Patterns:Which class interval has the highest frequency?
(Answer: Tallest bar)
How many observations are in the 30–39 class?
(Answer: Read height of that bar)
Estimate the total number of items from the histogram.
(Answer: Sum of all bar heights)

Common Mistakes:Treating histogram like a bar chart (looking for gaps)  
Using bar width instead of height to judge frequency

Objective 4: Compare Frequencies Using a HistogramExplanation:
Compare bars visually and numerically to answer comparison questions.Key Points:Taller bar → higher frequency  
Equal heights → equal frequencies  
Difference = taller bar height – shorter bar height

Visual Type: bar-comparison
Visual Items:  Two highlighted bars for comparison  
Arrow showing difference

Example Objective Question Patterns:Which interval has fewer observations: 20–29 or 40–49?
(Answer: Compare heights)
How many more students are in the modal class than the 50–59 class?
(Answer: Difference in heights)

Common Mistakes:Guessing visually without reading scale  
Misreading axis scale

SUBTOPIC 3: Bar ChartObjective 5: Interpret a Bar ChartExplanation:
Bar charts show discrete or categorical data with separated bars.  Each bar represents one category  
Height/length shows frequency/value  
Gaps between bars indicate separate categories

Key Points:Read labels on x-axis (categories)  
Frequency/value from y-axis  
Compare categories easily

Visual Type: separated-bars
Visual Items:  Category labels on x-axis  
Bar heights with values

Example Objective Question Patterns:Which subject recorded the highest number of passes?
(Answer: Tallest bar)
What is the total number of students surveyed?
(Answer: Sum of all bar heights)

Common Mistakes:Treating bar chart like histogram (expecting touching bars)  
Ignoring category labels

Objective 6: Extract Comparative Information from a Bar ChartExplanation:
Compare bars to find differences, totals, or rankings.Key Points:Difference = taller bar – shorter bar  
Use exact scale reading

Visual Type: comparison-bars
Visual Items:  Highlighted bars for comparison  
Arrow showing difference

Example Objective Question Patterns:How many more students passed Mathematics than English?
(Answer: Difference in heights)
Which two subjects have the same number of passes?
(Answer: Equal bar heights)

Common Mistakes:Using wrong units from axis  
Misreading scale

SUBTOPIC 4: Pie ChartObjective 7: Interpret a Pie ChartExplanation:
Pie chart shows parts of a whole (total = 360° or 100%).  Larger sector → larger proportion  
Angles or percentages given

Key Points:Total angle = 360°  
Fraction = sector angle / 360°  
Percentage = fraction × 100

Visual Type: circle-sectors
Visual Items:  Sector angles labelled  
Category names

Example Objective Question Patterns:Which activity occupies the largest portion?
(Answer: Largest sector)
What fraction of the total is spent on food?
(Answer: Angle/360°)

Common Mistakes:Ignoring angle sizes  
Mixing up labels

Objective 8: Calculate Proportions from a Pie ChartExplanation:
Convert angles to fractions, percentages, or actual values.Key Points:Actual value = (angle/360°) × total  
Percentage = (angle/360°) × 100

Visual Type: fraction-circle
Visual Items:  Angle → fraction → percentage mapping  
Example calculation

Example Objective Question Patterns:If 90° represents students who play football, what percentage do they represent?
Answer: (90/360) × 100 = 25%
Total students = 720. If science sector is 120°, how many students like science?
Answer: (120/360) × 720 = 240

Common Mistakes:Dividing by 100 instead of 360  
Forgetting total value

Final High-Level Tips for JAMB Success:Always read titles, labels, and scales carefully – most errors come from misreading  
For frequency tables: sum frequencies for totals/ranges  
Histograms: touching bars, height = frequency  
Bar charts: separated bars, categories on x-axis  
Pie charts: angles → fractions → percentages/values  
Practice 50+ interpretation questions from past papers  
Never guess visually – always read exact values

